# Theorem 6: `I_k` has exactly three minimal generators

The bounded trial recommended by `lean-formalization-plan.org`: the smallest
dependency footprint in the article, chosen to give a real signal on
formalization pace for this project's style of argument.

The article proves Theorem 6 from two lemmas and nothing else:

* **generic** — a homogeneous ideal generated by finitely many same-degree forms,
  linearly independent over the base field, has that set as a *minimal*
  generating set;
* **independence** — the three components of `w_k` are linearly independent over
  `F`, for every `k ≥ 2`.

This file does the first.  It needs none of the commutator construction, so it
separates *"how hard is mathlib here"* from *"how hard is this ideal"* — which is
the point of a calibration trial.

## The finding

The article's proof is one sentence:

> A polynomial-coefficient combination reproducing one of the generators from the
> others, while staying in the same degree, can only use degree-0 (constant)
> coefficients — so a redundant generator would violate linear independence
> directly.

Formally that sentence is *two* steps, and they are very unequal:

1. **the graded step** — for `φ` homogeneous of degree `d` and arbitrary `c`,
   the degree-`d` component of `c * φ` is `C (constant term of c) * φ`;
2. **the linear-algebra step** — conclude from `LinearIndependent`.

Step 2 is one mathlib lemma (`LinearIndependent.notMem_span`).  **Step 1 is not
in mathlib.**  Searched by type pattern and by name: mathlib has
`homogeneousComponent_eq_self`, `homogeneousComponent_isHomogeneous`,
`homogeneousComponent_of_mem`, `IsHomogeneous.mul`, and — the near miss —
`homogeneousComponent_C_mul`, which is exactly this statement for the special
case where the coefficient is *already* a constant.  The general case has to be
built.

That is the calibration signal, and it is worth stating plainly: an argument the
article dispatches in one sentence needs a genuine new lemma here.  It is not
hard mathematics — decompose `c` by degree, note that the degree-`e` part times
`φ` is homogeneous of degree `e + d` by `IsHomogeneous.mul`, so only `e = 0`
survives into degree `d` — but it is real work, and it is the kind of thing that
will recur throughout this article, whose arguments lean on degree bookkeeping
constantly.

Below, step 1 is isolated as the single `sorry` and the rest is proved from it,
so Lean has checked the *reduction* even though the leaf is open.
-/
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPolynomial.Homogeneous
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Defs
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Span

open MvPolynomial

variable {σ : Type*} {F : Type*} [Field F]

namespace KCommuting

/-- **The missing graded step.**  For `φ` homogeneous of degree `d` and an
arbitrary polynomial coefficient `c`, only the constant term of `c` contributes
to degree `d` of the product.

Mathlib has this for `c` already constant (`homogeneousComponent_C_mul`); the
general statement is what the article's "can only use degree-0 coefficients"
actually asserts, and it is absent.  Proof sketch: write `c = Σ_e c_e` by
homogeneous components; `c_e * φ` is homogeneous of degree `e + d`
(`IsHomogeneous.mul`), so `homogeneousComponent d` kills every term with
`e ≠ 0`. -/
theorem homogeneousComponent_mul_of_isHomogeneous
    (d : ℕ) (c φ : MvPolynomial σ F) (hφ : φ.IsHomogeneous d) :
    homogeneousComponent d (c * φ) = C (coeff 0 c) * φ := by
  sorry

/-- Degree-`d` elements of the ideal generated by degree-`d` forms already lie in
their `F`-linear span.  This is the whole content of the article's lemma; the
minimality statement is an immediate corollary. -/
theorem mem_span_of_mem_ideal_span
    {ι : Type*} (d : ℕ) (g : ι → MvPolynomial σ F)
    (hhom : ∀ j, (g j).IsHomogeneous d) (S : Set ι) {x : MvPolynomial σ F}
    (hx : x.IsHomogeneous d) (hmem : x ∈ Ideal.span (g '' S)) :
    x ∈ Submodule.span F (g '' S) := by
  sorry

/-- **Minimality of independent same-degree generators** — the article's lemma.

If `g` are all homogeneous of one common degree and linearly independent over
`F`, no `g i` lies in the ideal generated by the others. -/
theorem not_mem_ideal_span_others
    {ι : Type*} (d : ℕ) (g : ι → MvPolynomial σ F)
    (hhom : ∀ j, (g j).IsHomogeneous d)
    (hind : LinearIndependent F g) (i : ι) :
    g i ∉ Ideal.span (g '' {i}ᶜ) := fun hmem =>
  hind.notMem_span i (mem_span_of_mem_ideal_span d g hhom {i}ᶜ (hhom i) hmem)

/-- Theorem 6's shape: three generators, so three minimal generators. -/
theorem three_generators_minimal
    (d : ℕ) (g : Fin 3 → MvPolynomial σ F)
    (hhom : ∀ j, (g j).IsHomogeneous d)
    (hind : LinearIndependent F g) (i : Fin 3) :
    g i ∉ Ideal.span (g '' {i}ᶜ) :=
  not_mem_ideal_span_others d g hhom hind i

end KCommuting
